\section*{Introduction}

In modern wireless communication systems, signal transmission is complicated by environmental effects that alter the received signal. These effects not only include perturbation by additive noise, but also introduce reflections and scattering through objects in the environment, creating multiple signal paths between the transmitter and receiver. This phenomenon is known as multipath propagation, with typical observations of clusters of strong signals from specific reflection paths, especially in urban areas.

For BT's own research into development of future systems, finding methods for estimating the number and properties of these clusters is of great interest. Specifically, this may allow for improved signal preconditioning, helping the recovery of the ground truth signals and increasing channel capacity. 

In this report, we make a preliminary assessment of whether these properties can be retrieved from data already available in such a system. Our study 
focuses on a simplified model, using the measurements of the signals at multiple receivers to identify clusters. We propose a method that estimates the angular distribution of signals incident a receiver array, thereby identifying clusters as peaks in this distribution. Two variants of this approach are compared against two existing direction-of-arrival (DOA) algorithms, which are limited to estimating specific angles of incoming waves.

We structure the report as follows. We first present our interpretation of the problem, motivating a simplified two dimensional model of multipath propagation. We then provide further context with an introduction to two existing algorithms for estimating direction-of-arrival (DOA): Prony's method \cite{ouibrahim1986generalized} and ESPRIT \cite{roy2002esprit}. In \emph{Methodology, Approaches and Preliminary Results}, we introduce the discretised horizon method for approximating the angular distribution of sources and evaluate its performance alongside Prony's method and ESPRIT. We then propose objectives for future research and conclude with a discussion of the challenges and importance of continued research in this area.


%We first provide a background on the problem of identifying clusters from the H-matrix, and introduce our interpretation of the problem and the toy model created in the work of the ITT. We then introduce two existing methods for finding DoA's: Prony's method and ESPRIT.


