\section*{Problem Formulation and Background}

% \subsection{Context}


%In modern wireless communication, the problem of recovering the original message is an inverse problem, with the forward operator formulated as a linear transformation, subject to noise.

In modern wireless communication, an array of transmitters send signals to an array of receivers, subject to perturbations through the environment. The problem of recovering the original message is an inverse problem, with the forward operator formulated as a linear transformation, subject to additive noise.
%The channel gain is modelled as a linear transform subject to addititive noise,
A single instance of this process can be modelled as
\begin{equation}
    \label{eqn:forward-model}
    \mathbf{y} = H\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{n},
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{C}^M$ is the measurement at an array of $M$ receivers, $\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{C}^{N}$ is the original message sent by the transmitter array, $H\in\mathbb{C}^{M \times N}$ is the channel gain matrix and $\mathbf{n}\in\mathbb{C}^N$ is the noise.\\

The channel gain matrix is estimated frequently through protocols which send known signals to the receiver for comparison.
This allows the receiver to approximate the original message. In addition, the transmitter uses this information to precondition the message for easier reconstruction at the receiver. This process occurs frequently, with higher rates when the user is traversing/changing environment.\\

Knowing the characteristics of reflections in the channel are of keen interest, and may allow better preconditioning in future systems (citation). 

