\subsection*{Interpretation and Toy Model}
%We now introduce our interpretation of the problem and the toy model created in the work of the ITT. 
Here we present a toy model abstraction of the problem. 
%We make the following assumptions:
% uniform linear array

We represent the forward operation as a combination of two mappings (see Figure \ref{fig:scattering_model}). Firstly, a one-to many map from the transmitters to the last reflection points, followed by a many-to-one map from the last reflection points to the receivers. We initially propose the following formulation
\begin{equation}
    \label{eqn:two-maps}
    \mathbf{y} = F_2(F_1(\mathbf{x}) + \mathbf{n}_1) + \mathbf{n}_2
\end{equation}
where $F_1$ represents the scattering of the original signals prior to their last bounces and $F_2$ represents the dependency of the received signals on the last bounces.

%We are interested in the properties of clusters of signals 
We are interested in the properties of the last bounces, and how they impinge on the receiver array. Thus, we focus on the second mapping
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{y} = F_2(\mathbf{z}) + \mathbf{n},
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{z}$ is a vector of scattered signals.



