% Now, we treat the last bounces as phantom sources, with their distance from the receivers large enough to assume they sit in the far field with respect to the width of the array. This assumption allows us to consider only planar waves in our model, with interference effects given by linear combinations (superposition).

Now, we treat the last bounces as an array of phantom sources with the assumption that they sit in the far field with respect to the width of the array.
This gives rise to a model of measured data $\mathbf{y}$ as a superposition of planar waves with additive noise. That is, we formulate each $y_m$, $m=1,...,M$ as
\begin{equation}
    \label{eqn:scalar-model-0}
    y_m = \sum_{i=1}^{\tilde{N}} z_i \exp\big\{j(m-1)\sin(\theta_i)\big\} + n_m, \qquad m = 1,...,M,
    % what is A what is z
\end{equation}
where $j = \sqrt{-1}$, $\tilde{N}$ is the number of phantom sources, with $\theta_i$ and $z_i\in\mathbb{C}$ the direction of arrival and complex envelope of the signal from the $i^\text{th}$ phantom source, respectively.
The full measurement across the receivers can be written as a linear operation with an $M \times \tilde{N}$ complex Vandermonde matrix,% $B$,
\begin{equation}
    \label{eqn:lin-op}
    \mathbf{y} = B\mathbf{z} + \mathbf{n}
\end{equation}
where $B_{mi} = \exp\{j(m-1)\sin(\theta_i)\}$.
%In the following, we typically expand 
Moreover, we can write (\ref{eqn:scalar-model-0}) explicitly in terms of real and imaginary components by expanding the complex envelope $z_i = A_i e^{j\phi_i}$, 
\begin{equation}
    \label{eqn:scalar-model-1}
    y_m = \sum_{i=1}^{\tilde{N}} A_i \exp\big\{j(m-1)(\phi_i+\sin(\theta_i))\big\} + n_m, \qquad m = 1,...,M.
    % what is A what is z
\end{equation}
In this paradigm, the clusters of reflections can be identified by finding $\{\theta_i\}_{i=1}^{\tilde{N}}$. In the literature, this is known as a direction of arrival (DOA) or angle of arrival estimation problem. In the remainder of this section, we outline two existing algorithms used for DOA estimation. 

In the following section, we will introduce an alternative method (the discretised horizon approach) which aims to identify clusters by instead estimating the distribution of DOAs across the horizon.

