\subsection*{Motivation for a Discretised Horizon Method}
% In Prony and ESPRIT, we have so far seen DOA-finding methods which can  produce specific directions towards last reflection points. However, we are interested estimating clusters of reflection points, not necessarily the specific location of individual paths. Moreover, the effectiveness of methods are constricted by the number of receivers and require an otherwise-determined guess at the number of paths. This motivates us to instead consider finding the distribution of DOA, rather than individual points. 

In Prony and ESPRIT, we have so far examined DOA-finding methods that estimate specific directions corresponding to the last reflection points. However, our primary interest is to identify clusters of reflection points, rather than precise locations of individual phantom sources. Moreover, these methods are constrained by the number of receivers and require an otherwise-determined guess at the number of paths. This motivates us to instead consider recovering the full distribution of DOAs rather than individual point estimates.

Assuming incident waves are sampled from a distribution $z(t)\sim\nu(\theta)$, we have
% mike formulation
\begin{equation}
    y_m(t) = \int_{-\pi}^\pi z(\theta,t)\exp\big\{j\sin(\theta)(m-1)\big\}d\theta + n_m(t)
\end{equation}
In the case of $\nu(\theta)$ being a sum of dirac deltas, this reduces to the sum seen in (\ref{eqn:scalar-model-0}).

However, assuming $z(\theta, t)$ instead follows a continuous distribution, we are motivated to find a histogram approximation to this distribution. With this objective, we proposed the following discretised horizon method in the ITT - which additionally has no systematic constraint on the minimum number of receivers.



% In order to capture the density (other properties?) of clusters, we are interested in producing a distribution over the horizon of incoming signals.

% In working towards this aim during the ITT, we came up with the following scheme.

\subsection*{Discretised Horizon Approach used in the ITT}
To build our histogram, we discretise the horizon $[-\pi/2, \pi/2]$ into $K$ bins, centred at
\begin{equation}
    \theta_k = \bigg(\frac{k-1}{K-1}-\frac{1}{2}\bigg)\pi, \qquad k=1,...,K.
\end{equation}
Let $\mathbf{u} \in \mathbb{R}^K$ be a vector of amplitudes in each bin. We have the following forward problem
\begin{equation}
    \label{eqn:disc-1}
    \mathbf{y} = A(\boldsymbol{\theta})\mathbf{u} + \mathbf{n}
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{\theta} = (\theta_1,..., \theta_K)^T$ and $A(\boldsymbol{\theta})$ is an $M\times K$ complex matrix with 
\begin{equation}
    a_{mk}(\theta_k) = \exp\big\{j \sin(\theta_k) (m-1)\big\}, \quad m=1,...,M, \ k = 1,...,K. 
\end{equation}
That is, the $k^\text{th}$ column of $A(\boldsymbol{\theta})$ represents the receiver measurements from unit source at angle $\theta_k$, with phase adjusted to be zero at $y_0$.

All that remains is to solve the inverse problem of finding the histogram $\mathbf{u}$ from measurements $\mathbf{y}$ and the forward model in (\ref{eqn:disc-1}). A linear least squares (LS) approach finds $\mathbf{u}$ such that
\begin{equation}
    \underset{\mathbf{u}\in\mathbb{C}^K}{\text{argmin}} ||A(\boldsymbol{\theta})\mathbf{u}-\mathbf{y}||.
\end{equation}
An alternative compressed sensing (CS) approach minimises
\begin{equation}
    \underset{\mathbf{u}\in\mathbb{C}^K}{\text{argmin}} ||A(\boldsymbol{\theta})\mathbf{u}-\mathbf{y}|| + \lambda||\mathbf{u}||.
\end{equation}
% Finally, we retrieve an estimate of the histogram $\mathbf{u}$ by solving the inverse problem in (\ref{eqn:disc-1}).

% \subsubsection{Inverse Problem}
% % solving eqn:disc-1
% We address the inverse problem of finding the histogram $\mathbf{u}$ from measurements $\mathbf{y}$ and the forward model in (\ref{eqn:disc-1}).

% Our objective is to find the histogram $\mathbf{u} \in \mathbb{R}^K$, given measurements $\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{C}^M$. We do so by solving (\ref{eqn:disc-1}) as an inverse problem.

