\subsection*{Numerical Experiments}
Here we present preliminary results found in the ITT for the discretised horizon method, Prony's method and ESPRIT. We generate synthetic noiseless data according to our toy model in (\ref{eqn:scalar-model-0}) with various sampling methods used.

\subsubsection{Prony's Method and ESPRIT}
\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    % \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{3_sources_prony.png}
    \includegraphics[width=0.4\linewidth]{7_sources_prony_v2.png}
    \includegraphics[width=0.4\linewidth]{plot_esprit.png}
    \caption{Reconstructions for Prony's method with 16 receivers and 8 sources, and ESPRIT for 8 receivers and 4 sources with $T=1000$.}
    \label{fig:prony}
\end{figure}

In figure \ref{fig:prony}, see that Prony's method roughly approximates the correct range of angles (the true values are sampled at random), and has some success in matching amplitudes (also sampled at random) of the incoming waves. However, the angle estimates clearly do not line up exactly. A likely factor towards this poor performance to the root finding component of Prony's method, which may become numerically unstable for many sources.

On the other hand, ESPRIT shows much more promising angle recovery, even when those angles are perturbed by noise over time. Although, we must attribute part of the comparative success of ESPRIT to the lower number of sources to be dealt with.


\subsubsection{Discretised Horizon}

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    % \includegraphics[width=0.4\linewidth]{reconstruction_comparison40.png}
    \includegraphics[width=0.4\linewidth]{reconstruction_comparison20.png}
    % \includegraphics[width=0.4\linewidth]{reconstruction_comparison15.png}
    \includegraphics[width=0.4\linewidth]{reconstruction_comparison10.png}
    \caption{Reconstructions for discretised horizon method with 20 bins and 2 sources. Left: 20 receivers, right: 10 receivers.}
    \label{fig:disc}
\end{figure}

To test the discretised horizon method in a similar manner to Prony's method and ESPRIT, we first consider the problem of DOA estimation with 2 sources. We generate noiseless data according to (\ref{eqn:scalar-model-0}) with $\theta_1 = -11\pi/38$, $\theta_2 = 9\pi/38$ and amplitudes 2 and 3, respectively. We then apply the discretised horizon method with 20 bins, comparing the results for 20 receivers and 10 receivers in figure \ref{fig:disc}.

We see that, for 20 receivers, the discretised horizon method performs adequately in identifying two clusters. The compressed sensing version of the algorithm also produces a closer estimate, affirming our expectations for the regularising term to promote sparsity in the solution. However, in each case there are overestimates for surrounding points and the LS solution even mistakenly increases at the tails of the estimated distribution. This motivates further examination of the method with stronger regularisation terms.

Moreover, it is worth considering the coarseness in the recovered distribution of amplitude, particularly for LS. Without sufficient regularisation, we expect that further sources may be absorbed into other peaks. This may not be such an issue for the task of identifying clusters, but marks the discretised horizon method as inherently lower resolution than ESPRIT and Prony's method.

As for the case of 10 receivers, results are much less promising. LS completely fails to capture the correct shape of the distribution and although CS is closer, it mistakenly troughs at the exact angle of the second source and exhibits spurious peaks at the tails. This again shows stronger regularisation is needed, although this may not make up for errors expected from solving the underdetermined system, such as the underestimate for the second source.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{reconstruction_20.png}
    \caption{Reconstruction of a distribution of sources with LS discretised horizon: 20 bins, 20 receivers and 100 sources. The true distribution is given by two added Gaussians.}
    \label{fig:disc-2}
\end{figure}

In figure \ref{fig:disc-2}, we test the LS discretised horizon method on a continuous distribution of sources, where the true angular distribution is given by the sum of two Gaussian curves. Although the method captures the basic qualitative structure of two dominant peaks or clusters, it also produces erroneous fluctuations across the horizon and significantly overestimates the intensity of the second peak relative to the first. This behaviour highlights the numerical instability of the LS solution, and we defer a investigation into the effectiveness of regularisation in this context to future work.


\section*{Future Work} % research goals
% \begin{itemize}
%     \item Literature search on discretised horizon, or maybe just ask Anvar
%     \item how many bins are possible
%     \item To what extent can the discretised horizon overcome the "double the receivers as sources" threshold
%     \item How can we quantitatively compare these methods
% \end{itemize}

We propose four main objectives for future work.
% \begin{enumerate}
%     \item Search for other methods for finding horizonal distribution
%     \item Search for algorithms for finding number of DOA's
%     \item Extension to the H-matrix data
%     \item Review state-of-the-art, ensure relevancy to BT
% \end{enumerate}

\textbf{1. Search for other methods for finding the angular distribution.}
Many existing DOA algorithms, such as Prony's method, ESPRIT, MUSIC, and Pisarenko's algorithm, estimate specific angles corresponding to phantom sources \cite{ouibrahim1986generalized}. In continuing our aim of approximating an angular distribution of sources, future work should investigate whether these algorithms can be modified to instead output a distribution. For instance, if the current DOA estimates are interpreted as Dirac delta functions, it may be possible to incorporate a suitable convolution within the algorithm to obtain a smoothed distribution.

\textbf{2. Search for algorithms for finding number of DOAs.} 
One of the more prominent constraints of Prony's method and ESPRIT is their reliance on user input for the number of directions. In future work, these algorithms would become much more relevant to the problem of identifying clusters if they were coupled with methods for estimating the number of directions.

\textbf{3. Extension to the H-matrix data.} 
A key drawback of our study so far has been the exclusion of H-matrix data, which was originally proposed as the primary source of data for the challenge. Several DOA algorithms operate directly on H-matrix data, a prominent example being MUSIC \cite{schmidt1982signal}. Further numerical experiments with MUSIC and its modern extensions—such as Gradient-MUSIC \cite{fannjiang2025optimality} and DeepMUSIC \cite{elbir2020deepmusic}—would therefore be desirable.

Moreover, further analytical work is needed to extend the discretised horizon method to take in H-matrix data as input. This extension is not straightforward and it may instead be necessary to modify existing H-matrix-based algorithms to produce a histogram output, as in point 1.

\textbf{4. Review state-of-the-art, ensure relevancy to BT.}
It is difficult to establish within the context of a short ITT the scope of already available methods and the specific gap in application most relevant to an industry partner. Therefore, the most immediate future work will be focused on a reviewing state-of-the-art methods for direction finding. Through continued active communication with BT, we aim to identify the most relevant approaches with the precise application in mind.


% \subsection{Other methods for finding horizonal distribution}
% We believe it would be pertenant to conduct a literature review on the litany of DOA algorithms, and worth investigating whether convolutions can be added to convert dirac delta outputs to a continuous distribution.

% \subsection{Algorithms for finding number of DoA's}
% A notable weakness of Prony's method and ESPRIT is the need for user input in number of sources.

% One possible algorithm is provided in

% \subsection{Review State-of-the-art, ensure relevancy to BT}
% Identify the gap: for such a field, it is difficult to establish within a week what is/isn't available.


\section*{Discussion}
We conclude this report with three final points on research in this area.

\textbf{1. Challenges of future research in this area.}
Reflecting on our suggestions on future work, perhaps the main challenge in continuing this line of research is developing a broad review of relevant methods and the contextual challenges of a real-world wireless communications system. Once this context is established, we expect the work of this report to be quickly superseded with more complex numerical experiments, investigating more relevant methods. Our aim will be to study approaches that directly operate on the H-matrix, taking into account its stochastic properties and the limitations of physical equipment.

\textbf{2. Points for clarification.}
In future, close collaboration with BT will be required for an accurate assessment of these challenges, the behaviour of physical equipment and the research goals most pertinent to BT's interest. Considering the poor performance of these algorithms in cases with few receivers, we would be interested in whether future work could take advantage of multiple phones in the same vicinity collaborating.

\textbf{3. Drawbacks from experiments.}
We expect future work to also superseded the present approach in comparing discretised horizon, Prony's method and ESPRIT. The experiments conducted are not consistent in their data generation, and only serve to showcase example implementations and their systematic constraints, rather than quantitatively compare performance.



