\chapter*{Appendix}

\subsection*{Notes on Subspace Based Methods}
We give a brief introduction to subspace based methods for DOA estimation - a class of algorithms ESPRIT belongs to alongside MUSIC.
These aim of these algorithms is to recover DOAs from the eigenstructure of the spatial covariance matrix, given by
\begin{equation}
    \label{eqn:spatial-cov}
    R = \mathbf{y}\mathbf{y}^\dagger = BZB^\dagger + \sigma^2 I \in \mathbb{C}^{M \times M}
\end{equation}
where $Z = \mathbf{z}\mathbf{z}^\dagger \in\mathbb{C}^{\tilde{N}\times\tilde{N}}$ is the covariance matrix of signals, $\sigma$ is the noise power and $B$ is the Vandermonde matrix given in (\ref{eqn:lin-op}). We assume $\tilde{N} < M$.

It can be shown that (see \cite{ouibrahim1986generalized}):
\begin{itemize}
    \item The minimum eigenvalue of $R$ is $\sigma^2$ with multiplicity $M-\tilde{N}$.
    \item The corresponding $M-\tilde{N}$ eigenvectors are orthogonal to the columns of $B$.
\end{itemize}

Thus, once this noise space is determined (as the span of these eigenvectors), we can acquire DOAs by finding the eigenvectors of $R$ orthogonal to the noise subspace.

\subsubsection{ESPRIT as a subspace method}
As an alternative to (\ref{eqn:u-signal}), we can retrieve $U_\text{signal}$ from $R$, as done in the previous section. For time-dependent measurements, we write $R$ as its expected value
\begin{equation}
    R = \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{y}(t)\mathbf{y}(t)^\dagger] \approx 
    \frac{1}{T}YY^\dagger =
    % \frac{1}{T}U\big[\Lambda \quad 0\big]V V^\dagger \big[\Lambda \quad 0\big]^\dagger U^\dagger = 
    \frac{1}{T}U\Lambda^2 U^\dagger
    = \frac{1}{T}U_\text{signal}\Lambda_\text{signal}^2 U_\text{signal}^\dagger + \frac{1}{T}U_\text{noise}\Lambda_\text{noise}^2 U_\text{noise}^\dagger.
\end{equation}

Comparing with (\ref{eqn:spatial-cov}), we identify
\begin{equation}
    U_\text{signal}\Lambda_\text{signal}^2 U_\text{signal}^\dagger = BZB^\dagger, \qquad U_\text{noise}\Lambda_\text{noise}^2 U_\text{noise}^\dagger = \sigma^2 I.
\end{equation}
i.e., the columns of $U_\text{signal}$ span the same subspace as the steering matrix $B$.



